From: picowl <picowl@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] scene: update a buffer node when its opacity changes

A surface commit sets the opaque region and the size of its scene node before
it swaps the buffer (surface_reconfigure). The update which the new size
triggers therefore sees the previous buffer, and for an opaque one the whole
node, in its new size, occludes what is below it. When the buffer is then
swapped for one that is not opaque, nothing updates the node again, so the
visible regions of the nodes below keep the hole: they are culled where the
new buffer only covers them translucently, and the renderer draws that buffer
over whatever the frame buffer held.

This is what happens to a layer surface that grows from an opaque RGB565 bar
to an ARGB8888 bar with a translucent row, over a window that was already
there: the window disappears under the row. Update the node when the opacity
of the buffer changes.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 types/scene/wlr_scene.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/types/scene/wlr_scene.c b/types/scene/wlr_scene.c
--- a/types/scene/wlr_scene.c
+++ b/types/scene/wlr_scene.c
@@ -875,6 +875,16 @@
 			scene_buffer->buffer_height != buffer->height;
 	}

+	// What the node occludes depends on whether its buffer is opaque, and the
+	// regions of the nodes below it are only recomputed by an update. A
+	// surface commit changes the size of the node before it swaps the buffer
+	// (surface_reconfigure), so that update has seen the previous buffer: an
+	// opaque RGB565 one that has made the whole of the larger node occluding.
+	// The nodes below would stay culled under a translucent ARGB8888 buffer.
+	if (buffer != NULL) {
+		update = update || scene_buffer->buffer_is_opaque != wlr_buffer_is_opaque(buffer);
+	}
+
 	// If this is a buffer change, check if it's a single pixel buffer.
 	// Cache that so we can still apply rendering optimisations even when
 	// the original buffer has been freed after texture upload.
